Pesticide residues have raised serious public concern towards agriculture, environment and food safety. Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been employed as promising recognition and signal generation elements in sensors for pesticide detection. However, the general format of tiny particles with poor dispersity brings obstacles to detection operation and the improvement of sensing performance. Here, we report a sensor based on porous MOFs hybrid sponge for fluorescent-visible detection of methyl parathion. Benefiting from the inter­ mediate of adhesive and porous fibrin film, MOFs are loaded with good dispersion and accessibility, thereby endowing the sensor with a rapid response time of $10\mathrm{-min}$ , a wide linear detection range of $50{-}2500\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and a low limit of detection of $4.95~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the hybrid sensor presented superior durability and antiinterference ability to the detection in complex conditions, including organic solvents, acidic solution, high temperature, and even chemical interferences. This hybrid not only provides a new construction strategy for a nanomaterial-based sensor, but also permits a portable and durable route for the detection.
## 1. Introduction
Pesticides are globally utilized to adjust and protect the growth of crops. Nevertheless, abuse and misuse of pesticides and thereby tremendous amounts of toxic residues raised severe public concern to­ wards food safety, health and environment (Topping et al., 2020; Car­ atelli et al., 2022; Murcia-Morales et al., 2022). Specifically, organophosphorus pesticides (OPs), the most widely used pesticides, are highly neurotoxic, causing poisoning and even death at low dose level (Pundir et al., 2019). Therefore, sensitive and timely detection of OPs is crucial. So far, various laboratory-based analytical techniques, such as chromatography and spectrometry, contribute mainly to accurate, sen­ sitive and high-throughput detection. However, they require time-consuming procedures, precisely-controlled detection environ­ ment and expensive large-scale instruments, which are not applicable to the rapid and on-site detection (Zhang et al., 2022a,b). As the supple­ ment and substitution, sensors, particularly fluorescent-visible sensors, have become viable and potentially portable tools to detect OPs, and thus, have received intensive attention (Pundir et al., 2019; Shen et al., 2022).
The receptor, one of the essential elements of a sensor, significantly impacts detection performance. Compared with fragile biological re­ ceptors, nanomaterials are more robust against high-temperature, organic solvents, acidic/alkaline conditions, playing new roles in con­ structing integrated and durable sensors for practical applications (Li et al., 2022; Paschoalin et al., 2022; Xu et al., 2022). Among them, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn increasing consideration recently (Sun et al., 2021). Within the impressive advantages in large surface area, high porosity, abundant physic-chemical properties for the concentration, recognition and response, MOFs have been employed to detect various pesticides (Cheng et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2021). None­ theless, with their inherent nature as inorganic coordination materials, MOFs generally present as tiny powders with poor dispersity in the so­ lution, which not only brings inconvenience to operation, and more importantly, decreases the sensing performance, such as reliability and reproducibility (Jia et al., 2022). To improve the applicability of MOFs in sensing, fabricating MOFs into a robust macroscopic hybrid device as a integrated portable sensor is promising and highly demanded (Yao et al., 2022).
Ideally, a hybrid-based sensor should possess the following characteristics. 1) The loaded MOFs should be highly active with adequate amounts. 2) The device should be porous, not only to improve the accessibility of MOFs, but also to accelerate mass transfer, thereby improving the sensitivity and response speed of the sensor. The above conditions are determined by the preparation method. At present, it is mainly achieved by modifying MOFs on the pre-formed devices (Kim et al., 2017; Li et al., 2020) or shaping pre-synthesized MOFs into macroscopic devices (Moon et al., 2015; Zhu et al., 2016). As for the construction of a sensor, in the former route, the load of MOFs is limited by substrate inertness and the harsh preparation conditions (Zhang et al., 2022a,b). On the other way, the porous structure is challenging to achieve, and the activity of the MOFs generally suffers from the pore blockage (Tan et al., 2020).
Herein, we present a novel sensor based on MOF hybrid sponge for fluorescent-visible detection of methyl parathion (m-PT). The hybrid is composed of luminescent zirconium-based MOFs (Zr-LMOFs), fibrin and melamine sponge (MS), defined as Zr-LMOFs/fibrin/MS (Zr/F/MS), in which adhesive and porous fibrin film serves as the intermediate mod­ ifier for the load of Zr-LMOFs with high activity and accessibility for sensing (Fig. 1). Benefiting from the preparation method and novel structure, as well as the inherent fluorescent response of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs, the sensor exhibited satisfactory performance for the detection of m-PT, and the signal can be realized by naked eyes. Moreover, compared with the sensor based on Zr-LMOF particles, ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ performed well in complex detection environments, proving its excellent potential adaptability in practical application scenarios. This method and the corresponding hybrid sponges are promising in the development of novel sensors with good portability and durability for the detection.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and characterizations of Zr-LMOFs
Owing to the strong $\mathrm{Zr(IV){-}O}$ bonds between the hexanuclear zir­ conium nodes and the carboxylate linkers, $z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOFs possess remarkable chemical and thermal stabilities, having attracted consid­ erable attention recently (Liu et al., 2021). In our previous work, water-stable $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs were proved to detect m-PT with high sensitivity and rapidity (He et al., 2019). In this work, using benzoic acid as a modulator, we successively synthesized $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs with high crystal­ linity in a shorter time. As shown in Figs. 2a and 2b, the as-prepared Zr-LMOFs presented as surface-rough spheres with the diameter of $100{-}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Under daylight, Zr-LMOFs were white microcrystalline powders and could be easily dispersed in water under sonication (Inset 1 of Fig. 2b). The suspension presented bright blue fluorescence with the excitation of ultraviolet light (Inset 2 of Fig. 2b). The maximum exci­ tation and emission wavelengths of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs were $310\mathrm{nm}$ and $405\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. S1a and S1b). Additionally, we investigated the inherent stabilities of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs thoroughly, since they are significant to the practicability of the sensor. Thermogravimetric analysis indicated that $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs possessed excellent thermal stability within a high decomposition temperature up to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S2). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs were unchanged after incubation in water at room temperature for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ and at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , demonstrating excellent water stability (Fig. 2c). Besides, the photostability of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs is adequate to rapid detection, as proved by the remained fluorescent intensity (FI, over $95\%$ ) after continuous irradiation with 15 min (Fig. S3). The above stabilities lay the foundation for applying $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs towards fluorescent detection in both solution and high temperature environment.
After optimization of the conditions, (Fig. S4 and Fig. S5) the fluo­ rescent response of the Zr-LMOFs towards m-PT was conducted by incubating $5\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\mathrm{Zr}$ -LMOFs with different concentrations of m-PT for $5~\mathrm{min}$ . As shown in Fig. S6, the FI of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs decreased as the con­ centration of m-PT increased. The quenching rate (QR) at $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ exhibited a good linear relationship to the logarithmic molar volume of the concentration of m-PT (Fig. 2d). The linear detection range (LDR) was $25{-}1500~{\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}}$ , and the LOD was calculated to be $3.15~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (S/N ${}=3{\mathrm{:}}$ , both of which are better than or comparable to the reported similar materials (Kumar et al., 2014; Yang et al., 2018, 2020; He et al., 2019), demonstrating the extraordinary responsive activity of the prepared Zr-LMOFs towards m-PT.
## 2.2. Preparation and characterizations of Zr/F/MS
To entirely explore the energetic properties of MOFs for sensing, we
L. Zhang et al.
integrated discrete MOFs powders into a hybrid device, using adhesive and porous fibrin film as an intermediate. Fig. 1 illustrates the prepa­ ration process for ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ . Firstly, fibrin formed in the MS with the insitu bio-polymerization of fibrinogen (Fg). In detail, Fg would be free in the pore or adsorb to the framework of MS with their hydrophobic groups under squeezing (Chong et al., 2015). During the blood-coagulation-mimicking process triggered by thrombin (Thr), all Fg molecules, would be nonselectively cleaved by Thr, and then, inter­ connect together to form fibrin. The adhesion of fibrin would help the formation and stabilization of the structure. The above product was washed with extensive water to remove of residuary phosphate saline and reactants and thereby obtaining fibrin/MS (F/MS). This process is of importance to create a mild and clean environment for the subsequent loading of hihgly-active $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs. Finally, Zr/F/MS was prepared by mixing F/MS with Zr-LMOFs suspensions for physical adsorption. Benefitting from abundant interactions between Zr nodes/benzene rings and various amine acid residues in fibrin, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs would adsorb to fibrin spontaneously under drastically shock (Kato et al., 2019; Loosen
et al., 2022).
SEM images were collected to characterize the morphology and microstructure. MS presented as a surface-smooth framework with pores of $50{-}200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S7a). As for F/MS (Fig. S7b), porous film filled the pore and adhered on the framework, which was the typical morphology of fibrin (Lammert et al., 2016; Zhang et al., 2019). In the ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ (Fig. 3a and Fig. S7c), particles that attached to fibrin film were observed, and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy mapping proved the distribution and the characteristic element of Zr-LMOFs (Fig. S8). The porous structure and the exposed $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs would facilitate the direct interaction between $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs and m-PT, thereby improving the sensi­ tivity and response speed of the sensor. Besides, Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy confirmed the co-existence of MS (Fig. S9), fibrin and $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs, while XRD patterns verified the remained crys­ talline structure of $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs in Zr/F/MS (Fig. S10).
Fluorescent spectra were utilized to record the adsorption of $z\mathbf{r}.$ - LMOFs (Fig. 3b). Both pure MS and $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{F}/\mathbf{M}\mathbf{S}$ present no fluorescent char­ acteristics under the excitation of ultraviolet light. After directly shaking MS in $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Zr-LMOFs suspension for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , the product (defined as $\mathbf{Zr/MS})$ still possessed a negligible fluorescent emission peak after washing, reflecting the weak interaction between MS and $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs. In contrast, within only 3-h shaking, $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ presents a prominent char­ acteristic emission peak around $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the intensity was about 72.2-fold than that of $z_{\mathrm{r/MS}}$ . This confirms the successful load and the remained fluorescent characteristic of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs. Furthermore, the adsorption of different concentrations of $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs to F/MS was also imaged and monitored (Fig. 3c). Clearly, all ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ samples exhibited uniform blue fluorescence under ultraviolet light, and the FI increased with the increased concentration of $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs. These reflect the uniform distribution of $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs, stemming from the uniformity of fibrin across the framework of MS, as well as the physical adsorption method for the load. As for the detailed adsorption kinetics, the $\mathrm{FI}_{405~\mathrm{nm}}$ of all $Z\mathrm{r/F/MS}$ samples increased within the increase of shaking time and achieved almost saturation at $3\mathrm{h}$ . The $\mathrm{FI}_{405\mathrm{nm}}$ of $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ that obtained with 20, 50, 100, 250 and $500~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs suspensions were $3070\pm409$ , $10336\pm1224$ , $21662\pm3728$ , $31897\pm4291$ and $53305\pm1895$ , respectively. The above results demonstrated the high adsorption capacity of fibrin towards $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs, and implied a quick adsorption process, profiting from the porous structure of F/MS. Noted that the $\mathrm{FI}_{405\ \mathrm{nm}}$ of ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ that obtained by 250 and $500~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ Zr-LMOFs suspensions decreased slightly after 12-h shaking, which might be attributed to the aggregation-induced desorption of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs particles.
## 2.3. Sensing performance of Zr/F/MS towards m-PT
To improve the sensing performance of Zr/F/MS, several important assay parameters were optimized. The quantity of the receptor is one of the most important parameters that determines the performance. Hence, we optimized the content of $z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs in ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ via controlling the preparation. 3-h was firstly selected for the adsorption of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs according to Fig. 3c. The concentration of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs was further opti­ mized by comparing QRs of different ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ samples towards $200~\upmu\up g$ $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ m-PT. As shown in Fig. 3d, the $\mathrm{FI}_{405\ \mathrm{nm}}$ of $Z\mathrm{r/F/MS}$ that prepared within 20, 50, 100 and $250~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{zr}$ -LMOFs suspensions before and after the incubation of m-PT were $3099\pm574$ and $1906\pm239$ , 11018 $\pm\ 455$ and $6891\pm437$ , $21659\pm3732$ and $17403\pm4294$ , $30284\pm$ 4604 and $25401\pm268$ , respectively. The QRs were calculated as 0.3815 $\pm\ 0.0375$ , $0.3732\pm0.0655$ , $0.2018\pm0.0607$ and $0.1670\pm0.0888$ , respectively. The changing trend was similar to the response of ZrLMOFs towards m-PT in Fig. S4. To balance the LDR and LOD, $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was selected as the optimized concentration to prepare ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ for sensing. Besides, the incubation time of $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ and m-PT was also optimized to be 10-min (Fig. S11).
In a practical detection application scenario, complex conditions (e. g., organic solvents, acidic/alkaline solution, high temperature, and even chemical interferences) are common, coming from the pretreatment procedures of practical samples or the surrounding environ­ ment. These might disturb the normal operation of the sensor, especially via affecting the receptors. Consequently, durable and robust receptors and sensors are highly-demanded.
To investigate the applicability of the sensor, extreme conditions, including $\mathsf{p H}5$ solution, $10\%$ EtOH, and heating at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , were firstly selected. Noted that alkaline condition was not selected, due to the hydrolysis-caused instability of m-PT (Lan et al., 2019). The stabilities of $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ under these conditions were confirmed by the nearly-unchanged FI (Fig. S12). These profit from the chemical and thermal stabilities of Zr-LMOFs, as well as the structure stability of $Z\mathrm{r/F/MS}$ . Further, we investigated and compared the responses of $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs suspensions towards m-PT in these conditions. As shown in Fig. $4\mathrm{c}$ , the QRs of ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ under the above conditions were $0.36\pm0.0141$ , $0.3429\pm0.0032$ , $0.4262\pm0.0586$ and $0.3125\pm$ 0.0165, respectively. The almost unchanged responsive values confirm the high reliability and durability of $Z\mathrm{r/F/MS}$ . In contrast, the QRs of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs suspensions were $0.5488\pm0.0124$ , $0.5875\pm0.0320$ , 0.5866 $\pm\:0.1095$ and $0.1328\pm0.0163$ , respectively, indicating the remained normal functions of $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs in $\mathrm{pH}5$ and $10\%$ EtOH except $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The sharply decreased response of Zr-LMOFs at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was speculated to the decreased surface area and sites for the binding of m-PT, which was caused by the accelerated aggregation of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -LMOFs particles under heating (Fig. S13). This result displays the significance of fabricating MOFs into a hybrid device for sensing, not only for portability and easy operation, but also durability in practical applications.
Besides, the anti-interference abilities of $\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}$ towards other potential chemical interferences were also evaluated. Fig. 4d exhibited the QRs of Zr/F/MS towards nine types of OPs, five types of non-OPs and four types of ions (the concentration of pesticides is $500~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and ions are $1\mathrm{mM}$ ). Among them, only m-PT resulted in an apparent fluorescent quenching, indicating good anti-interference ability of $z\mathrm{r/F/MS}$ . This was attributed to the high binding affinity of $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-OH}}$ towards phosphorus (Yang et al., 2018), and the possible quenching mechanism of fluores­ cence resonance energy transfer between electron-rich $z\mathbf{r}$ -LMOFs and the electron-withdrawing nitro group $\left(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)$ in m-PT (He et al., 2019).
Finally, the recovery tests with spiked rice, apple and tap water samples were performed to demonstrate the reliability of ${\mathrm{Zr/F/MS}}$ in real samples. As shown in Table 1, satisfactory recoveries ranging from $90.1\%$ to $107.5\%$ were obtained, indicating a good accuracy for the determination of m-PT in agricultural and environmental samples.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a portable sensor based on MOFs hybrid sponge was developed for the fluorescent-visible detection of OPs. Compared with large-scale detection instruments in laboratory, this sensor exhibited distinct advantages in portability and simplicity for operation. Inte­ grating the advantages of preparation method and the porous structure, the sensor provided a rapid response time of $10\mathrm{min}$ , a LDR of 50–2500 $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu\mathbf{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ , and a low LOD of $4.95\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the sensor exhibited good durability and reliability towards complex interferences, including organic solvent, acidic solution, high temperature, and chemicals. This is hardly achieved by sensors based on particles, since which generally suffer from aggregation-caused inactivation. The above suggests a great potential of the MOFs-based hybrid for not only the sensitive and reli­ able detection, but also integrated and portable devices for various applications.
